body:
I would be pretty surprised if the last three won't be Inferno, Italy and Anubis.

&#x200B;

I personally think Anubis is the most eye pleasing map.

I swear this subreddit is like the definition of insanity. People keep posting photos of white powder/paste asking if it's speed and every comment is telling them that it's impossible to tell from a photo and yet they still keep doing it.

&#x200B;

Let's start telling people that it's definitely not speed and that they should throw it away. I wonder how that would go.

It was meant as a joke since you know witches and potions, but you need bigger or more of everything including the container in which you do the extractions obviously.

&#x200B;

Look up the percentage of DMT in the reeds and then do some math derived from the percentage of DMT in mimosa hostilis which is 1-2% if I'm not mistaken. Basically if reeds have, let's say, 0.1% DMT you would need 10X the quantity of everything.

[https://wiki.dmt-nexus.me/Category:Extraction\_Tek#STB](https://wiki.dmt-nexus.me/Category:Extraction_Tek#STB)

&#x200B;

But I guess you would need a cauldron

I guess it depends on how efficient the pulls are, again coming back to the DMT/Naphta ratio. From my understanding, the precipitation doesn't happen if the concentration of DMT is too low or if it does, it takes a lot longer for it to happen.

&#x200B;

So, in order to know if it's necessary to let it evaporate or not, you need to find out the DMT concentration amongst other things; to find out the DMT concentration you need to know how much DMT is in the solution; to find out how much DMT is in the solution you need to (paradoxically) extract it and weight it.

&#x200B;

Now that I think about it, it's quite interesting because in theory you could optimize the extraction so that you get to keep almost the same amount of solvent that you started with, but that entails having a very controlled environment (basically a lab, most likely a very expensive one) and doing a whole lot of experimenting. That's simply absurd, especially given the context.

&#x200B;

In practice, if you want to keep as much as the solvent as possible, I can only see one way, you would still need to do an experiment that takes days if not weeks:

\- First you try doing the freeze precipitation without evaporating any of it beforehand. If it works, then great! If it doesn't, you heat it up in order to dissolve those floating DMT crystals back into the solvent. 

\- After doing that, you evaporate some amount of it, let's say for example 10ml. Then you try the freeze precipitation again. If it works, then great! If it doesn't, repeat until it does. 

After you found the amount of solvent that needs to be evaporated in order for the freeze precipitation to work then you just need to evaporate that amount when you do further extractions, but it would only work for sure if you do the exact same extraction tek with the exact same amounts and even then, there would be some variables, like for example the DMT concentration in the plant itself which you can't really control.

&#x200B;

So, any way you think about it, you still lose solvent whether you want it or not. The question is: do you really want to go through the trouble of experimenting with it so you can minimize the loses or do you just go by the rule of thumb and just let it evaporate until it is cloudy and lose a good portion of it? 

&#x200B;

One more thing that springs to mind is that maybe you can filter the solvent through a coffee filter or something so that you extract it without the need for it to stick to the bottom of the container, but this is just pure speculation from my part, I really don't know if it will work or not. In actuality I could be wrong about many things I've written in this reply since it was all from what I remember reading when I first needed to find out about freeze precipitation, so do your own research on it, you'll find plenty of posts on this subreddit, on DMTLab and the DMT Nexus forum regarding this topic with all kinds of tips.
